{"pagination":{"maxPageableSet":"10000","numFound":1,"query":"name=Graeber&limit=10&title=Fragments of an Anarchist Anthropology","limit":10,"start":0},"items":{"mods":{"@version":"3.8","titleInfo":{"title":"Fragments of an anarchist anthropology "},"name":{"@usage":"primary","@type":"personal","namePart":"Graeber, David,","role":[{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"creator"}},{"roleTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"author"}}]},"typeOfResource":"text","genre":{"@authority":"rdacontent","#text":"text"},"originInfo":[{"place":{"placeTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"ilu"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"marccountry","#text":"Illinois"}]},"dateIssued":{"@encoding":"marc","#text":"2004"},"issuance":"monographic"},{"@eventType":"publication","place":{"placeTerm":{"@type":"text","#text":"Chicago :"}},"agent":{"namePart":"Prickly Paradigm Press :","role":{"roleTerm":"publisher"}},"dateIssued":"2004."}],"language":{"languageTerm":[{"@type":"code","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"eng"},{"@type":"text","@authority":"iso639-2b","#text":"English"}]},"physicalDescription":{"form":[{"@authority":"marcform","#text":"print"},{"@type":"media","@authority":"rdamedia","#text":"unmediated"},{"@type":"carrier","@authority":"rdacarrier","#text":"volume"}],"extent":"105 pages ; 18 cm"},"abstract":{"@type":"Summary","#text":"Everywhere anarchism is on the upswing as a political philosophy--everywhere, that is, except the academy. Anarchists repeatedly appeal to anthropologists for ideas about how society might be reorganized on a more egalitarian, less alienating basis. Anthropologists, terrified of being accused of romanticism, respond with silence ... But what if they didn't? This pamphlet ponders what that response would be, and explores the implications of linking anthropology to anarchism. Here, David Graeber invites readers to imagine this discipline that currently only exists in the realm of possibility: anarchist anthropology. -- Publisher description."},"tableOfContents":{"@type":"Contents","#text":"Section 1: Why are there so few anarchists in the academy? -- Section 2: Graves, Brown, Mauss, Sorel -- Section 3: The anarchist anthropology that almost already does exist -- Section 4: Blowing Up Walls -- Section 5: Tenets of a Non-existent Science -- Section 6: Globalization and the Elimination of North-South Inequalities -- Section 7: Anthropology (in which the author somewhat reluctantly bites the hand that feeds him)."},"note":{"@type":"statement of responsibility","#text":"David Graeber."},"subject":[{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":["Political anthropology","Philosophy"]},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"Anarchism"},{"@authority":"lcsh","topic":"State, The"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":["Anthropologie politique","Philosophie"]},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"Anarchisme"},{"@authority":"rvm","topic":"État"},{"@authority":"aat","topic":"anarchism"},{"@authority":"fast","@href":"(OCoLC)fst00808387","topic":"Anarchism"},{"@authority":"fast","@href":"(OCoLC)fst01069206","topic":["Political anthropology","Philosophy"]},{"@authority":"fast","@href":"(OCoLC)fst01131943","topic":"State, The"},{"@authority":"fes","topic":"Anarchismus"},{"@authority":"fes","topic":"Anthropologie"},{"@authority":"gnd","topic":"Anarchismus"},{"@authority":"gnd","topic":"Anthropologie"},{"@authority":"gtt","topic":"Anarchisme"},{"@authority":"gtt","topic":"Sociale aspecten"},{"topic":"sociale antropologie"},{"topic":"social anthropology"},{"topic":"politiek"},{"topic":"politics"},{"topic":"filosofie"},{"topic":"philosophy"},{"topic":"samenleving"},{"topic":"society"},{"topic":"anarchisme"},{"topic":"anarchism"},{"topic":"Political Philosophy"},{"topic":"Politieke filosofie"}],"classification":[{"@authority":"lcc","#text":"GN492 .G73 2004"},{"@edition":"22","@authority":"ddc","#text":"306.2"},{"@authority":"bcl","#text":"73.70"},{"@authority":"rvk","#text":"CC 7800"},{"@authority":"rvk","#text":"LB 39000"},{"@authority":"rvk","#text":"LB 31999"},{"@authori